\title{LDscnR: LD-Aware Complexity Reduction and Outlier-Region Detection for Genome Scans}
\description{
Linkage-disequilibrium-aware genome scans. Estimates chromosome-wise LD decay and derives a local-LD weight for every marker, then partitions markers into LD-coherent clusters from genotypes alone, without reference to phenotype or environmental covariate, extending the linkage disequilibrium network approach of Kemppainen et al. (2015, Molecular Ecology Resources 15:1031-1045) and the complexity-reduction clustering of Li et al. (2018) \doi{10.1111/1755-0998.12893}. Each retained cluster is reduced to one coherent multilocus genotype pattern or cluster-level statistic and tested as a unit in place of every constituent marker, so that redundant markers in strong LD no longer dominate the test count or the interpretation of the rejection set. Testing is engine-agnostic: the user supplies observed and permuted association p-values from whatever method they prefer, so the same clusters can be paired with different engines and null-construction schemes. Significant clusters can subsequently be assembled into regions for reporting, a post hoc step kept distinct from the hypotheses subjected to multiple-testing correction. In the primary high-gene-flow simulations, this approach improved region-level precision relative to marker-wise testing while reducing recall. Structure-aware phenotype nulls provide a separate check of whether observed discovery counts exceed counts generated by population structure alone. Comparisons with independent stickleback evidence evaluate reported regions. Earlier applications of LD clustering to this problem, including the consistency-C-score formulation of Fang et al. (2021) \doi{10.1093/molbev/msab144}, remain available in the package but are no longer the primary method.
}
